1318 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/P 73 0 R /F 4/M(D:20240817121215+08'00')/HighDpi false/QuadPoints[ 83.52 175.2 142.304 175.2 83.52 155.76 142.304 155.76]/Rect[ 83.52 155.76 142.304 175.2]/T(þÿÿÿ)/Subj(Highlight)/CreationDate(D:20240817121212+08'00')/C[ 0.427451 0.921569 0.427451]/CA 1/NM({9dedef1e-9b43-40e8-b327-aee93d6fc290})/Popup 1319 0 R /AP<</N 1320 0 R >>/Contents(Understanding "Reinforce":\n\n    Reinforce in this context refers to something that strengthens or encourages a certain behavior. In psychology, a "reinforcer" is anything that increases the likelihood that a behavior will be repeated. For example, praise, affection, rewards, or even attention can act as reinforcers.\n\nApplication in Relationships:\n\n    In a healthy relationship, positive reinforcements are things like love, affection, approval, or support. These are given freely and encourage positive behaviors, like kindness or cooperation.\n\n"A Reinforce for the Manipulated":\n\n    The Manipulated: This refers to the partner who is on the receiving end of the manipulation.\n    The Reinforce: This is something that the manipulated partner values or desires, such as affection, approval, or support. In a healthy relationship, receiving this reinforce would make the partner feel good and would naturally encourage them to keep behaving in a way that pleases their partner.)>>
endobj
